{"result": {"response": {"id": "resp_04fe6aedefd4df620169bb332e6ab48191a32916072b749e30", "created": 1773876014, "model": "gpt-5.2-2025-12-11", "object": "chat.completion", "system_fingerprint": null, "choices": [{"finish_reason": "stop", "index": 0, "message": {"content": "{\"title\":\"Stoma-Based Diversions: Permanent Colostomy and Indiana Pouch\",\"summary\":\"This community centers on surgically created abdominal openings (stomas) used for diversion procedures and the associated management methods. A key pathway describes permanent colostomy creation, where stool exits through a stoma and is collected via a colostomy bag. A second pathway describes continent urinary diversion (the Indiana pouch), where a stoma is used primarily as an access site for intermittent catheterization, enabled by a one-way valve and sometimes covered with a bandage. A combined approach—double-barreled wet colostomy (DBWC)—connects urinary and fecal diversion to a single abdominal stoma while keeping outputs separated until exit. Indiana University appears as the originating institution for the Indiana pouch technique.\",\"findings\":[{\"summary\":\"The stoma is the central node connecting fecal and urinary diversion workflows\",\"explanation\":\"A stoma is defined as a surgically created opening on the abdomen that can serve as an external exit for stool after colostomy, an exit for urine in some urinary diversions, or an access site for catheter drainage in continent diversions [Data: Entities (2797); Relationships (9431, 10798, 13435, 13433, 13423,+more)].\\n\\nIts centrality is reflected in multiple relationships: stool exiting through the stoma after permanent colostomy, the stoma’s use in DBWC, and the stoma’s role as the access point for catheterization in the Indiana pouch setup [Data: Relationships (9431, 10798, 13435, 13455, 13423,+more)].\"},{\"summary\":\"Permanent colostomy creation routes stool to the abdominal surface and requires external collection\",\"explanation\":\"Permanent colostomy creation is described as diverting stool by connecting the end of the colon to the outside of the abdomen, producing a stoma and requiring a bag to collect stool [Data: Entities (6193, 2798); Relationships (14192, 14217)].\\n\\nIn the associated pathway, stool exits through the stoma and is then collected in a colostomy bag attached to the skin [Data: Relationships (9431, 14217); Entities (2797, 2798)].\"},{\"summary\":\"Colostomy bag function is explicitly tied to stool collection from the stoma\",\"explanation\":\"The colostomy bag is an external pouching system attached over the stoma to collect stool after colostomy, including when the colostomy is permanent [Data: Entities (2798); Relationships (14217)].\\n\\nThe relationships in the dataset reinforce the functional chain: stool exits through the stoma and is collected by the bag attached to the skin [Data: Relationships (9431, 14217); Entities (2797, 2798)].\"},{\"summary\":\"The abdominal wall is the anatomical site used to create the stoma\",\"explanation\":\"The abdominal wall is described as the body wall location where a stoma is formed in diversion procedures, including continent urinary diversion, providing the external opening connected to an internal channel or reservoir [Data: Entities (2026); Relationships (13423)].\\n\\nThis anchoring relationship—stoma creation as a small hole in the abdominal wall—situates the community’s procedures around a common anatomical interface point [Data: Relationships (13423); Entities (2026, 2797)].\"},{\"summary\":\"Continent cutaneous pouch (Indiana pouch) uses a stoma for intermittent catheter drainage rather than continuous external drainage\",\"explanation\":\"The continent cutaneous pouch (Indiana pouch) is a urinary diversion that creates an internal urine-holding pouch connected to a stoma via an intestinal segment; urine is drained several times daily by inserting a catheter through the stoma and one-way valve, without an external ostomy bag [Data: Entities (5911, 5926, 5908); Relationships (13455, 13435, 13447)].\\n\\nThe dataset specifically links the Indiana pouch to catheterization through the stoma and links the stoma to that catheterization activity, emphasizing that the stoma functions as an access site for periodic drainage [Data: Relationships (13455, 13435); Entities (5926, 5911, 2797)].\"},{\"summary\":\"A one-way valve is a key enabling component of the Indiana pouch system\",\"explanation\":\"A one-way valve constructed from intestine prevents urine from flowing out through the stoma in the continent cutaneous pouch diversion [Data: Entities (5908, 5911); Relationships (13447)].\\n\\nThis relationship indicates the Indiana pouch’s continence mechanism: the valve supports controlled drainage via catheterization rather than uncontrolled leakage through the stoma [Data: Relationships (13447, 13455); Entities (5908, 5926, 5911)].\"},{\"summary\":\"The stoma may be managed with simple coverings in continent diversion contexts\",\"explanation\":\"A bandage is described as a small covering that may be used to cover the stoma in a continent cutaneous pouch diversion [Data: Entities (5909); Relationships (13433)].\\n\\nThis links stoma management practices to the continent diversion pathway, distinct from fecal diversion where an external bag is used for collection [Data: Relationships (13433, 14217); Entities (5909, 2798, 2797)].\"},{\"summary\":\"Double-barreled wet colostomy (DBWC) consolidates urinary and fecal diversion to a single stoma while keeping outputs separated until exit\",\"explanation\":\"DBWC is described as a combined diversion technique used when both urinary and fecal diversion are needed; it uses a single abdominal stoma while keeping urine and stool separate until they exit and may reduce leaks compared with separate diversions [Data: Entities (4898); Relationships (10798)].\\n\\nThe explicit linkage between stoma and DBWC highlights a hybrid configuration within the community that connects the stoma concept to multi-stream diversion design [Data: Relationships (10798); Entities (2797, 4898)].\"},{\"summary\":\"Indiana University is identified as the origin point for the Indiana pouch technique\",\"explanation\":\"Indiana University is identified as the institution where surgeons developed the continent cutaneous pouch technique commonly called the Indiana pouch [Data: Entities (5903); Relationships (13430)].\\n\\nThis provides institutional provenance for one of the community’s central diversion methods, tying the named technique to its development context [Data: Relationships (13430); Entities (5903, 5911)].\"}],\"rating\":3.2,\"rating_explanation\":\"Impact severity is low-to-moderate because the community reflects clinical surgical and device-management concepts with no provided indicators of illegality, malicious intent, or broader societal harm beyond medical significance.\"}", "role": "assistant", "tool_calls": null, "function_call": null}, "provider_specific_fields": {"native_finish_reason": "stop"}}], "usage": {"completion_tokens": 1409, "prompt_tokens": 3353, "total_tokens": 4762, "completion_tokens_details": {"accepted_prediction_tokens": null, "audio_tokens": null, "reasoning_tokens": 0, "rejected_prediction_tokens": null, "text_tokens": null}, "prompt_tokens_details": {"audio_tokens": null, "cached_tokens": 1536, "text_tokens": null, "image_tokens": null}}, "service_tier": null}}}